<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dorchester, Dorset</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Durnovaria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dorchester is the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a> in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It grew from Durnovaria, the Roman town that became the administrative centre of the Durotriges, close to the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hillfort of Maiden Castle. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tolpuddle-martyrs">Tolpuddle Martyrs</a> were tried there in 1834, and the novelist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-hardy">Thomas Hardy</a>, who lived nearby, portrayed it as Casterbridge.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dorchester-dorset.jpg" alt="Dorchester, Dorset" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alex Liivet a derivate work by MrPanyGoff · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Town_Pump_and_Corn_Exchange_-_Dorchester.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dorchester stands on gently sloping ground beside the south bank of the River Frome, about 7 miles (11 km) north of Weymouth, south of the Dorset Downs and north of the South Dorset Ridgeway. Growth to the north and north-east is limited by the river's floodplain and watermeadows. The chalk hills of the ridgeway carry over five hundred ancient monuments, including barrows, stone circles and hillforts, and many finds from the area are displayed in the Dorset Museum in the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest settlements lay about 2 miles (3.2 km) south-west of the modern centre near Maiden Castle, a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hill fort that was one of the most powerful settlements in pre-Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>; different tribes lived there from about 4000 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> defeated the local tribes by about 70 CE and set up a garrison from which grew the town they called Durnovaria. It is recorded in the 4th-century Antonine Itinerary and became a market centre, a road junction and staging post, and one of the twin capitals of the Durotriges.</p><p>Around 790 the first recorded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raid of the period came ashore on the Isle of Portland. Beaduheard, the king's reeve in Dorchester, rode out with a few men, took the newcomers for merchants and was killed with his companions. By 864 the area was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> who called themselves Dorsaetas, and the town's name passed through Dornwaraceaster to Dorchester.</p><p>Fires struck the town in 1613, 1622, 1725 and 1775; the first destroyed 300 houses and two churches, All Saints and Holy Trinity. After the Monmouth Rebellion the town was the site of the Bloody Assizes, presided over by Judge Jeffreys. In 1833 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tolpuddle-martyrs">Tolpuddle Martyrs</a> founded the Friendly Society of Agricultural Labourers; because members swore an oath, they were arrested and tried in the Shire Hall, with cells beneath the courtroom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dorchester is the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, with a weekly market, a hospital, schools and a college serving the surrounding area. It has 293 listed structures, among them the Church of St George, with a late 11th-century south door, the Church of St Peter, mostly of 1420 to 1421, Shire Hall, Max Gate and the Roman town house. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-hardy">Thomas Hardy</a> lived at Max Gate and made Dorchester the model for Casterbridge in The Mayor of Casterbridge.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dorchester, Dorset” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The territory became the civitas of the Durotriges with its centre at <b>Durnovaria</b>, modern Dorchester, within Roman Britain.</blockquote><a href="/state/durotriges">Durotriges</a></li></ul></section>
